{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>One who strikes a man and he dies.</b> Rashi's comment: since it is stated ואיש כי יכה כל נפש, I might think this applies to striking without death. And if you ask: where do we find it said that for striking without death one is executed — behold, even if he caused the loss of a limb, he pays only the value of the limb. And some explain with difficulty: when he made him a treifah. Or: when he struck him with something lethal and he did not die — as it is said, to impose the death penalty, since it was not within his power that he survived, it comes to teach us." "<b>One who strikes a man and he dies.</b> And this section is juxtaposed to the section of the slave, for it is habitual that a man strikes his slave, and sometimes he kills him; therefore it says <b>he shall surely be put to death</b> — it is not like striking a Canaanite slave."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "סד״א resolved as שנאמר; קמ״ל as קא משמע לן, standard talmudic idiom. The logical flow of the last clause is compressed and the rendering follows the text hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:20.846173934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}